NOTICE — Router Phase 2 (governed per-task routing)

The routing patterns implemented in lib/ (deterministic codemod pre-filter,
cost-shaped Thompson-sampling bandit per complexity bucket, structural
output verifier with a tier-escalation ladder, decision-record shape,
provider circuit-breaker/failover concepts) were studied from:

    RuFlow — https://github.com/ruvnet/ruflo
    Copyright © 2024–2026 ruvnet
    Licensed under the MIT License.

RuFlow was used as a behavioral reference only. This is a clean-room
implementation: no RuFlow source code is reproduced, copied, or derived
from — behavior was extracted into ~/workspace/router-phase2/
ruflo-behavior-spec.md and re-implemented under Kingmaker's governance
chain (Intent → Evidence → Governance → Decision → Authorization →
Audit), with outcomes driven by the structural verifier rather than
self-reported success.

The MIT License applies to the studied reference. This implementation is
Ronin Inc's own work.

Part 2 (federation + memory-with-audit + drift checkpoints) additionally
adapts these studied patterns:

  - Federation skeleton: Ed25519 node identity, capability-gated trust
    ladder, policy engine (message caps, claim types, delegation budgets)
    — adapted with enforce-only authorization, a session challenge
    handshake that runs on the live path, a human-consent gate on first
    contact and on trust promotion, and a PII pipeline whose prefix-
    leaking "hash" is replaced by redaction / keyed HMAC.
  - Memory provenance field, pattern confidence counters, and temporal
    decay — adapted with provenance made mandatory (writes without it
    are rejected), every write logged to the hash-chained audit chain,
    provenance-based revocation, and a default TTL.
  - Drift checkpoints have no upstream counterpart (the reference has
    zero drift control); that module is original Ronin Inc work.

Part 3 (guardrails + doctor + rollback) additionally ports/adapts:

  - The strong path validator (canonicalize + allowlist prefixes +
    blocked names/extensions) and the terminal env-var denylist —
    ported as-is; the spec judges both genuinely effective.
  - The AIDefence injection-pattern screen — ADAPTED, per the spec's
    own judgment that a regex gate is decoration: here it is an
    Evidence signal inside the governed router. A flagged task loses
    its fast lanes and routes frontier-tier only; the screen never
    authorizes and never vetoes. The mod screen's invisible/bidi-char
    stripping prep is ported into the screen.
  - The doctor contract — stable check IDs, {id,status,detail,fix?}
    JSON shape, ok iff no check fails (exit 1 on any failure).
  - Rollback — composed from the reference's two byte-restoring
    mechanisms (migration backup + memory checkpoints) into one
    command, with snapshot manifests signed under the helpers-manifest
    pattern (pinned public key, private key out-of-tree, fail-closed
    verify-then-copy). The witness signing scheme (forgeable by
    construction, drift-tolerant) is deliberately NOT ported.
  - Guardrails are wired into governed-router.mjs, not bolted on:
    the injection screen runs on every route (Evidence stage), and
    the runner receives a secret-scoped sandbox env built from the
    router's envScope — never the host environment.
